<p>Mina and Tariq can each see the last place in a class and both book it. Their requests can finish without a database error, with no negative counter and no duplicate booking identifier. The error is in the combination: the shop has promised two places when it has only one.</p>
<p>A <strong>transaction</strong> groups database work into a change that commits together or is rolled back (discarded) together. It gives us a boundary for fixing that race, but we still have to decide what belongs inside it and how competing decisions are protected. Those choices determine whether a successful response means a booking the shop can honour.</p>
<p>This article builds on the tables, keys and first competing buyers in <a href="/review/relational-trial-3/">Relational databases</a>. The same shop also runs classes: remaining places play the part of product stock, and a reservation records who took a place. We will work through PostgreSQL 18, then identify where another engine needs a different argument. Finish this article before choosing a deeper article; it supplies the decisions those articles build on.</p>

