<h2 id="start-with-the-fact-that-must-remain-true">Start with the fact that must remain true</h2>
<p>An <strong>invariant</strong> is a rule that every accepted state must satisfy. For each class, remaining places must stay between zero and capacity, and <strong>remaining + number of reservations = capacity</strong>. A customer may book a class only once. Together these rules say more than “the counter is nonnegative”: every consumed place has an owner, and nobody owns two places through two clicks.</p>
<p>These are the starting rows. A primary key (PK) identifies a row; a foreign key (FK) refers to a row in another table. The reservation’s <code>owner_id</code> refers to customers: Mina is 1, Tariq 2 and Edda 3.</p>
<div class="table-scroll" role="region" tabindex="0" aria-label="Starting classes"><table><caption>classes</caption><thead><tr><th>class_id · PK</th><th>title</th><th>capacity</th><th>remaining</th></tr></thead><tbody>{classes.map(row=><tr>{row.map(value=><td>{value}</td>)}</tr>)}</tbody></table></div>
<p class="relation-link"><code>classes.class_id ← reservations.class_id</code></p>
<div class="table-scroll" role="region" tabindex="0" aria-label="Starting reservations"><table><caption>reservations</caption><thead><tr><th>request_id · PK</th><th>class_id · FK</th><th>owner_id · FK</th></tr></thead><tbody>{reservations.map(row=><tr>{row.map(value=><td>{value}</td>)}</tr>)}</tbody></table></div>
<p>Tariq already holds R7 in Mug painting, so that class has two places left. Tea tasting has its last place available. Each figure restarts from these rows unless it says otherwise; they are separate experiments.</p>

<h3 id="put-the-complete-change-inside-a-boundary">Keep ownership and the counter together</h3>
<p>A booking reduces <code>remaining</code> and inserts a reservation. <code>BEGIN</code> opens an explicit transaction; successful <code>COMMIT</code> accepts its work, and <code>ROLLBACK</code> discards it. A <strong>statement</strong> is one SQL command. Without an explicit boundary, PostgreSQL normally commits each successful standalone statement. Two statements can therefore become two independent promises.</p>
<p>Figure 1 starts with Mina booking Mug painting as R10. Her second click, R11, tries to book it again. Compare what happens when the decrement commits before the duplicate reservation is refused.</p>
<TransactionFigure scene="atomicity" number={1} />
<p>With one transaction, the failed second booking loses its decrement too. This all-or-nothing property is <strong>atomicity</strong>. Keep the whole invariant-changing operation inside that boundary. For a move, releasing the old place and taking the new one belong together; two commits can lose the original booking without acquiring its replacement.</p>
<p>A <strong>savepoint</strong> marks a place within a transaction to which some work can be rolled back. It is not a separate commit. Rolling back only the failed insert while retaining its decrement would recreate the broken state.</p>
<p>Rollback here restores transactional table changes. If bookings instead use PostgreSQL’s number-generating <strong>sequences</strong>, an allocated number is not returned when the transaction aborts; a gap does not mean a booking committed. Schema-changing commands also have engine-specific boundaries. In MySQL 8.4, <code>ALTER TABLE</code> implicitly commits preceding work, so a later rollback cannot undo that booking. Check the engine’s contract before mixing schema changes with bookings.</p>

<h3 id="let-the-database-reject-invalid-states-where-it-can">Give the database the rules it can enforce</h3>
<p>A <strong>constraint</strong> is a rule the database checks when storing a change. <code>CHECK (remaining BETWEEN 0 AND capacity)</code>, with non-null columns, rejects an impossible counter. <code>UNIQUE (class_id, owner_id)</code> rejects the second booking even when two requests both checked and found no earlier one. The primary key on <code>request_id</code> prevents two reservations sharing that identifier, and foreign keys reject unknown classes and customers.</p>
<p>The equality between remaining places and reservation count spans tables. PostgreSQL cannot enforce it with a <code>CHECK</code> on the class row. The transaction protocol must preserve it on every path: ordinary booking, cancellation, move, capacity change and staff import. This is the consistency in <strong>ACID</strong>: atomicity, consistency, isolation (the contract for overlapping work) and durability (survival after commit). Constraints and correct application logic supply the rules; the database cannot infer that a decrement needs a reservation.</p>

<h2 id="two-correct-looking-transactions-can-make-an-incorrect-result">Two transactions can sell the same place</h2>
<p>Return to Tea tasting. Mina asks for R8 and Tariq for R9. Each first reads <code>remaining = 1</code>, calculates <code>1 − 1 = 0</code>, then writes that calculated zero and inserts its reservation. Each transaction preserves the rule when run alone from the starting state. We use PostgreSQL’s <strong>Read Committed</strong> setting: ordinary queries see committed data as each command begins.</p>
<Sql code={unsafe} label="Unsafe PostgreSQL booking: write a value calculated from an earlier read" />
<p>The important question is whether the read still authorises the write. Step through Figure 2 with <em>Read, then overwrite</em>. Its lanes are a <strong>schedule</strong>: a chosen order of reads, writes and commits across independent transactions. The tables show the committed state, separate from pending work in each lane.</p>
<TransactionFigure scene="race" number={2} />
<p>Both commit, leaving two reservations and zero remaining places. This is a <strong>lost update</strong>: Tariq’s stale calculation replaces the value without accounting for Mina’s change. A row-value check sees zero, which is valid by itself. Wrapping both requests in transactions did not protect their decisions from one another.</p>
<p>Compare the result with running the transactions one at a time, a <strong>serial execution</strong>. Whichever goes second reads zero and refuses the booking. No serial order of these correct decisions accepts both. We now have a specific outcome the protection must exclude.</p>

<h2 id="choose-protection-for-the-invariant-you-actually-have">Put the condition inside the change</h2>
<h3 id="express-a-change-and-its-condition-together">Change the current row only while a place remains</h3>
<p>PostgreSQL can test <code>remaining &gt; 0</code> as part of decrementing the very row that holds it. The following complete statement inserts a reservation only for a successful decrement. <code>WITH taken AS (…) </code> names the rows returned by that update; the insert consumes them.</p>
<Sql code={guarded} label="One PostgreSQL statement: take a place and record its owner" />
<p>One returned <code>request_id</code>, followed by successful commit, accepts R8. No returned row means the class is missing or full; no reservation was inserted. If the application needs to distinguish those reasons, it needs a separate read and an honest allowance for subsequent change. Any insertion error fails the statement and rolls the decrement back too. Do not turn that error into success or a second independent insert.</p>
<p>Switch Figure 2 to <em>Conditional change</em>. At PostgreSQL <strong>Read Committed</strong>, its normal isolation setting, a competing update waits while Mina holds the row. After her commit it rechecks the condition against the changed row. Zero remaining places no longer qualify. This depends on the engine and statement contract. The downloadable <a href={worksheet} download="transactions-worksheet.zip">native worksheet</a> is an executable experiment that runs these schedules against PostgreSQL. Its recorded run observed Tariq’s wait and refusal.</p>
<p>The statement also needs a stable target: <code>class_id</code> is the unique identity of an existing class. Every booking uses this decrement-and-insert operation; cancellations restore a place only when deleting a real reservation, within the same transaction. Moves and capacity changes need corresponding complete protocols. Direct staff edits can still break the cross-table equality.</p>
<p>An alternative is an <strong>optimistic comparison</strong>: change the row only if it still has the value on which the decision was based.</p>
<Sql code={optimistic} label="Reject a stale last-place calculation by comparing the old value" />
<p>This is only the comparison step, not a complete booking: a successful change still needs ownership recorded in the same transaction. Zero rows require a new read and business decision. For a general edited record, compare an incrementing version column rather than one field that might change away and back. Here the condition <code>remaining &gt; 0</code> expresses the actual rule more directly.</p>
<p>Uniqueness protects a different race. Two simultaneous Mina requests for Mug painting have enough total capacity to decrement twice, but she may book only once. The unique constraint arbitrates that shared claim: a contender may wait for the first insert’s outcome and then receive a duplicate error. Roll back the failed booking; retrying the same duplicate indefinitely cannot make it valid.</p>

<h2 id="understand-the-isolation-contract-behind-the-choice">What a read sees, and what a lock protects</h2>
<h3 id="versions-and-locks-answer-different-questions">A view is not ownership</h3>
<p><strong>Isolation</strong> specifies how transactions may observe and interfere with each other. PostgreSQL keeps several <strong>row versions</strong>, the values before and after a change, so an ordinary reader can use a committed older value while a writer is working. A <strong>snapshot</strong> selects which versions that read can see. This is <strong>multiversion concurrency control</strong>, or MVCC.</p>
<p>At Read Committed, each ordinary query gets a new snapshot. A report can read one remaining place, another transaction books it, and the report’s next query finds a reservation while still holding its earlier counter in application memory. A changed reread is a <strong>nonrepeatable read</strong>; a changed set of matching rows is a <strong>phantom</strong>. Neither means PostgreSQL exposed uncommitted work, which would be a <strong>dirty read</strong>.</p>
<p>PostgreSQL <strong>Repeatable Read</strong> keeps the snapshot established by the transaction’s first data query or change, rather than refreshing it at each command. A report can then read related facts from one stable view. Its own changes remain visible to itself. Keeping a read stable, however, does not reserve the place it describes.</p>
<p>A <strong>row lock</strong> holds conflicting writers or locking readers back from the selected row. An ordinary PostgreSQL read usually continues using a permitted version. <code>SELECT … FOR UPDATE</code> requests such a lock; it must be inside the transaction whose later decision it protects. If a standalone locking statement commits immediately, its lock ends before the next statement can use it.</p>

<h3 id="coordinate-decisions-that-span-several-rows">Lock the fact on which every writer can meet</h3>
<p>For this variation, remove the remaining counter and calculate free places by counting reservations. Set Mug painting’s capacity to 2, with Tariq’s R7 as its only starting reservation. Locking the reservation rows found by a query does not reserve room against a new row. An empty set contains no rows to lock; even a nonempty set can gain a distinct reservation. Mina and Edda can insert R13 and R14, leaving three bookings despite a lock on R7.</p>
<p>A <strong>coordination row</strong> is a stable row all relevant writers agree to lock before deciding. The class row exists even when its reservation set is empty. At PostgreSQL Read Committed, acquire that row, then issue a <em>separate next command</em> to read capacity and count reservations, decide and change, keeping the lock through commit. That separate command gets a fresh snapshot after any wait. Combining a count with the locking command can retain a count from before the wait.</p>
<div class="table-scroll" role="region" tabindex="0" aria-label="Count before and after a lock wait"><table><caption>Illustrative schedule, checked by the worksheet: class 22 has capacity 2 and only R7 committed. Both sessions use Read Committed.</caption><thead><tr><th>Order</th><th>Mina · A</th><th>Edda · B</th></tr></thead><tbody>
 <tr><td>1</td><td>Locks class 22, then inserts R13 without committing.</td><td>—</td></tr>
 <tr><td>2</td><td>Keeps the lock.</td><td>Starts one command that locks the class and counts its reservations. Its snapshot sees R7, not pending R13; the lock waits.</td></tr>
 <tr><td>3</td><td>Commits R13 and releases the lock.</td><td>Acquires the lock, but that command returns count 1 from its earlier snapshot.</td></tr>
 <tr><td>4</td><td>—</td><td>A separate count command sees R7 and R13: count 2. The class is full, so R14 is refused.</td></tr>
</tbody></table></div>
<p>If A rolls back instead, R13 disappears. B’s separate count then sees only R7: one of two places is free, so R14 can be accepted. Acquiring the lock settles who may decide next; the next command supplies the view on which that decision depends.</p>
<p>The protocol also has to cover cancellation, moves, imports and capacity edits. A foreign key checks that the class exists; it does not enforce the count. <a href="/review/relational-next/locking/">Designing changes that coordinate with locks</a> develops the complete counted-reservation protocol, including missing rows, lock modes and every participating writer.</p>

<h2 id="a-repeatable-view-does-not-by-itself-prove-the-rule">Protect a decision spanning several rows</h2>
<p>Tea tasting must retain at least one available instructor. Lena and Ravi each have an instructor row, initially available. A departure counts available instructors and proceeds only if more than one remains before the change. Each departure is correct alone: the second person should see one and stay.</p>
<p><strong>Serialisability</strong> requires committed transactions to have an effect equivalent to some one-at-a-time order; PostgreSQL requests it with the <strong>Serializable</strong> setting. Figure 3 gives both transactions the same starting snapshot and lets each change its own row. Watch the result under Repeatable Read, then choose Serializable.</p>
<TransactionFigure scene="staffing" number={3} />
<p>PostgreSQL Repeatable Read accepted both departures in the native run. The failure is <strong>write skew</strong>: two decisions rely on shared facts but change different rows, so a same-row write conflict does not stop them. Lena’s decision requires Ravi still to be available; Ravi’s requires Lena. In a serial explanation each must come first, which is impossible.</p>
<p>PostgreSQL’s Serializable setting tracks which writes could change another transaction’s read result. Lena read Ravi as available, but Ravi changes that fact; Ravi’s read of Lena is undermined in the same way. PostgreSQL can reject this combination with a <strong>serialisation failure</strong>, error code (SQLSTATE) <code>40001</code>. A <strong>predicate</strong> is a condition selecting rows: here, available instructors for class 21. Tracking that read also covers a new row that would change the count. This checking can reject a transaction; it does not make every matching insertion wait.</p>
<Sql code={depart} label="Lena’s complete departure decision at PostgreSQL Serializable" />
<p>Ravi uses the same count with his own name. The application checks the count before sending the update, and treats acceptance as provisional until commit succeeds. Both departures and every other operation able to violate this instructor rule must use the contract and correct decision logic. A staff deletion, reassignment or bulk update must preserve an available instructor too.</p>
<p>In the selected native schedule Lena committed and Ravi’s commit failed. After rollback, Ravi started the entire transaction again, counted one and declined departure. The repair needs all three parts together: each decision preserves the rule when run alone, every relevant writer uses Serializable, and an aborted attempt restarts with fresh reads and decisions. A new attempt that blindly repeats the old “make Ravi unavailable” statement is serialisable SQL with incorrect business logic.</p>
<p>A common coordination row could instead make departures take turns; a stored available-instructor counter could make the condition a one-row change, provided every writer maintains it. Those alternatives exchange detection and retries for deliberate coordination or another maintained value. Use a conditional statement for a rule held in one row, a constraint for identities and unique claims, and a protocol covering the full dependency for a count or relationship across rows.</p>
<h3 id="stronger-isolation-can-reject-a-result-instead-of-waiting">Read the named engine’s contract</h3>
<p>The same isolation name need not imply the same protocol. These comparisons come from primary documentation; only PostgreSQL was run here.</p>
<dl class="contracts">
 <div><dt>PostgreSQL 18</dt><dd>Read Committed refreshes ordinary reads per command and rechecks a conflicting row update after waiting. Repeatable Read gives a stable view but can permit write skew; a conflicting row changed since that snapshot can instead produce <code>40001</code>. Serializable adds dependency checking and whole-transaction restart.</dd></div>
 <div><dt>MySQL 8.4, InnoDB</dt><dd>At Repeatable Read, ordinary consistent reads use a snapshot while locking reads use current data. Range locking can also protect gaps between keys. Query shape and indexes affect that coverage. Do not transfer PostgreSQL’s row-lock or snapshot reasoning without checking this contract.</dd></div>
 <div><dt>SQLite, default separate-connection behaviour</dt><dd>Only one writer proceeds at a time. Rollback-journal mode, which saves old contents before replacing them, can make readers and the writing phase exclude each other; WAL (write-ahead log) mode lets readers keep a snapshot while a writer commits. Upgrading an old read snapshot to a write can fail with <code>SQLITE_BUSY_SNAPSHOT</code>; restart the decision. <code>BEGIN IMMEDIATE</code> seeks writer ownership at the start and can itself be busy.</dd></div>
</dl>
<p><a href="/review/relational-next/isolation/">Reading and testing isolation guarantees</a> follows these read and write contracts in detail. Serialisability itself promises an equivalent serial order, not that transactions physically run without overlap or a universal order matching external clocks. Establish read freshness separately when replicas or explicitly older views enter the system.</p>

<h2 id="an-aborted-transaction-must-be-reconsidered-as-a-whole">When transactions wait or fail</h2>
<p>A wait is not a refusal: the holder may commit or roll back, after which the waiter can continue. A <strong>deadlock</strong> is a cycle of waits. Suppose Mina moves R8 from 21 to 22 while Tariq moves R7 in the opposite direction. Mina locks 21, Tariq locks 22, then each requests the other class. Neither can finish while keeping its first lock.</p>
<p>PostgreSQL breaks the cycle by aborting one transaction with <code>40P01</code>. Every move path taking the distinct classes in ascending order, 21 then 22, removes this particular cycle: Tariq waits before holding 22, leaving Mina able to finish. It does not remove cycles involving other rows or constraints. The native worksheet forces the two-lock deadlock and checks the victim’s failed state.</p>
<h3 id="retrying-one-statement-can-preserve-a-stale-decision">Restart the decision</h3>
<p>For a known deadlock or serialisation abort, roll back and reconsider the whole transaction: reads, calculations, decisions and writes. PostgreSQL errors inside an explicit transaction normally leave it failed; another ordinary statement then returns <code>25P02</code> until rollback. This article’s recipes use full rollback, without savepoint recovery. Other engines can roll back only a statement for some errors, so check the actual error scope.</p>
<p>A lock timeout means protection was not acquired in time; a duplicate customer booking means that business action is invalid. Neither means “class full”. Retry only outcomes for which a new attempt makes sense. Bound attempts by a request deadline and count, add increasing randomised delay under contention, and return an honest failure when that allowance runs out. A safety guarantee does not guarantee every request eventually succeeds.</p>
<p>Keep transactions short by validating input and doing unrelated work before <code>BEGIN</code>. Keep the decision’s reads and writes together. Waiting for a person or an external service while holding the class row blocks later bookings; splitting the booking into two commits changes correctness. <code>NOWAIT</code> can refuse a conflicting lock immediately, while <code>SKIP LOCKED</code> omits locked rows. An omitted class is not evidence that it is absent or full.</p>

<h3 id="losing-the-reply-does-not-prove-rollback">A missing reply leaves an unknown outcome</h3>
<p>Known aborts differ from a connection lost while <code>COMMIT</code> is in flight. The database may have committed R8 before the reply disappeared. Repeating a decrement as a new booking can consume another place. Keep the same request identity, look up its durable result through the authoritative database, and verify that owner and requested action match before interpreting it as success. A missing row while the original transaction is still running does not settle the outcome.</p>
<p>This is the purpose of an <strong>idempotent operation</strong>: repeating the same logical request does not repeat its effect. The unique R8 key is useful, but it needs an application protocol around pending work, payload matching and retained results. <a href="/review/relational-next/request-recovery/">Recovering database requests safely</a> owns that protocol. <a href="/review/relational-next/connections/">Managing connections and transaction lifetimes</a> explains how to finish or discard the owning connection after failure.</p>

<h2 id="commit-has-a-durability-boundary-and-a-system-boundary">What COMMIT can promise</h2>
<p><strong>Durability</strong> is the survival promise after commit. PostgreSQL normally waits for the transaction’s write-ahead log, its recoverable record of changes, to reach durable storage before acknowledging success. That requires storage which honours the flush and the relevant safeguards enabled. With <code>synchronous_commit = off</code>, success can arrive before the log is durable, so a crash can lose recent acknowledged work. Isolation does not choose this loss boundary.</p>
<p>A local durable commit is also not a backup, protection from an operator’s mistaken delete, or an automatic promise that a replacement server has the change. <a href="/review/relational-next/physical-storage/">How stored rows become physical work</a> follows the log and recovery; <a href="/review/relational-next/operations/">Operating a relational service</a> follows the recovery copies and deployment contract.</p>
<h3 id="a-database-rollback-cannot-undo-an-external-effect">The boundary ends at the database</h3>
<p>If the application sends Mina’s confirmation email before committing and then rolls back, the email still exists. Sending it after commit leaves another failure window: the process can die after storing the booking but before sending. Record the booking and an email job in the same database transaction instead. A <strong>transactional outbox</strong> is that table of work to deliver later.</p>
<p>A separate worker can deliver the job, but a crash after delivery and before recording success can make it deliver twice. The recipient needs a way to recognise repeated work, and payments need their provider’s corresponding operation identity and a way to check whether a payment completed. <a href="/review/relational-next/background-work/">Connecting transactions to background work and events</a> develops those external effects; an ordinary database rollback cannot retract them.</p>

<h2 id="check-the-argument-with-competing-sessions-and-failures">Test the schedule that could break the rule</h2>
<p>A sequential success proves little about concurrency. Fix the starting rows, pause both sessions after the reads that justify their decisions, then release the changes in the troublesome order. Record the version, isolation level, statements, results and commit or abort status. Where blocking matters, use an independent observer to establish a real server lock wait before releasing its holder.</p>
<p>For the conditional booking already shown, the worksheet controls three independent connections. These are its barriers: points it must reach before allowing the next action.</p>
<ol>
 <li><strong>A holds:</strong> Mina begins at Read Committed, runs the conditional statement and gets R8. The test withholds her commit; the change remains pending.</li>
 <li><strong>B submits:</strong> Tariq begins at Read Committed and sends the same statement for R9, owner 2. The test continues without waiting for B’s reply so it can observe B while B has no result.</li>
 <li><strong>Observer confirms overlap:</strong> a third connection queries PostgreSQL’s activity view, <code>pg_stat_activity</code>, for B’s recorded server process identifier and requires <code>wait_event_type = 'Lock'</code>. Failure to observe that wait fails the test; a delay alone is not evidence that B reached the contested row.</li>
 <li><strong>A releases:</strong> only after the observed wait does the test commit A. B finishes with no returned reservation, then commits.</li>
 <li><strong>Observer checks:</strong> a fresh query finds remaining 0 and exactly R8. Both the wait and <code>0 + 1 = 1</code> are required: the final rows alone could also come from a test that accidentally ran serially.</li>
</ol>
<p>Download and extract the <a href={worksheet} download="transactions-worksheet.zip">native worksheet bundle</a>, which includes the shared fixture and SQL in their required directory layout. With Python 3, Node and PostgreSQL 18 tools installed on macOS or Linux, run <code>python3 a03/verify.py 56140 &gt; native.json</code> from the extracted directory, choosing an unused port. <code>PG_BIN</code> can override its default Homebrew tools path. The script removes inherited PostgreSQL connection settings and creates its own temporary server. It connects through a private local socket (an endpoint inside its temporary directory) and checks the server’s identity before creating the test database. It stops its sessions and server and removes the temporary files even on an assertion failure.</p>
<details><summary>The exact schema and starting rows</summary><Sql code={setup+'\n'+seed} label="Canonical class-booking fixture used by the worksheet" /></details>
