 <li>PostgreSQL 18: <a href="https://www.postgresql.org/docs/18/functions-sequence.html">Sequence functions</a>: allocated numbers are not reclaimed after abort; <a href="https://www.postgresql.org/docs/18/wal-async-commit.html">Asynchronous commit</a>: the acknowledgement and crash-loss boundary.</li>
 <li>MySQL 8.4: <a href="https://dev.mysql.com/doc/refman/8.4/en/implicit-commit.html">Implicit commits</a>: schema commands that end preceding work; <a href="https://dev.mysql.com/doc/refman/8.4/en/innodb-transaction-isolation-levels.html">InnoDB isolation levels</a>; SQLite: <a href="https://www.sqlite.org/isolation.html">Isolation</a>: read modes, locking and restart choices that differ from the PostgreSQL example.</li>
 <li><a href="/review/relational-next/locking/">Designing changes that coordinate with locks</a>: the complete protocol for counted reservations, absent rows and multiple classes.</li>
 <li><a href="/review/relational-next/isolation/">Reading and testing isolation guarantees</a>: deeper visibility and dependency histories.</li>
 <li><a href="/review/relational-next/applications/">Building applications around a relational database</a>, <a href="/review/relational-next/request-recovery/">request recovery</a> and <a href="/review/relational-next/background-work/">background work</a>: ownership, uncertain outcomes and recoverable external effects.</li>
</ul>
